                <pb facs="0786" n="688" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg688"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1500: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Tenney</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-04T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 4, 1920—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 4—9:52 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>100. Referring to Department’s telegram of April 24, 2 p.m. After a consultation
                    with the Premier and with Russo-Asiatic Bank officials and Horvat, I conclude
                    that China does not intend to disregard former agreement with Russia though
                    ambitious to make Chinese influence more effective in the management of Chinese
                    Eastern Railway.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tenney</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
